<http://data.silknow.org/object/dd58a599-4d29-3799-b92f-0eba681493a1/observation/1>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/technical-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "Kimono of grey-green silk crêpe (chirimen) woven with a design of gold carp leaping over waterfalls of white and blue. The technique, with long floating supplementary wefts, is unusual for kimono fabric. Lining of dark blue silk."@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/dd58a599-4d29-3799-b92f-0eba681493a1> .

<https://sws.geonames.org/1861060/>
        a       ecrm:E53_Place .

<http://data.silknow.org/object/dd58a599-4d29-3799-b92f-0eba681493a1/observation/4>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "This kimono belonged to kabuki actor Ichikawa Danjūrō IX, one of the young men depicted in the print of\nthe acting family below. It is not known whether the garment was worn for performance or off-stage. The auspicious motif relates to a legend in which fish who are able to leap over a mighty waterfall are transformed into dragons. The story is a metaphor for male achievement and advancement.\n []"@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/dd58a599-4d29-3799-b92f-0eba681493a1> .

